<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Convention for a Democratic South Africa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1991–1992</span> · Also known as CODESA</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Convention for a Democratic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> (CODESA) was the multi-party negotiation on ending apartheid that opened near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannesburg">Johannesburg</a> in December 1991. It brought together the government, the ANC and many other parties, but its second plenary broke down in May 1992 over constitutional principles and political violence. Talks resumed in 1993 in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/multi-party-negotiating-forum">Multi-Party Negotiating Forum</a>, which agreed the interim constitution.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/convention-for-a-democratic-south-africa.jpg" alt="Convention for a Democratic South Africa" width="320" height="206" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carol M. Highsmith · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:F.W._de_Klerk,_left,_the_last_president_of_apartheid-era_South_Africa,_and_Nelson_Mandela,_his_successor,_wait_to_speak_in_Philadelphia,_Pennsylvania_LCCN2011634245.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
